  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f(x)=\\int x^3 \\sqrt{3-x^2} d x$. If $5 f(\\sqrt{2})=-4$, then $f(1)$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "MpI3N",
      "text": "$-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "2KdyW",
      "text": "$-\\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bAQoL",
      "text": "$-\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "vooBM",
      "text": "$-\\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\text { Let } 3-x^2=t^2 \\\\ & +x d x=-t d t \\\\ & f(x)=\\int\\left(3-t^2\\right) \\cdot t(-t d t)+c \\\\ & =\\int\\left(\\mathrm{t}^4-3 \\mathrm{t}^2\\right) \\mathrm{dt}+\\mathrm{c} \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{t}^5}{5}-\\mathrm{t}^3+\\mathrm{c} \\\\ & \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})=\\frac{\\left(3-\\mathrm{x}^2\\right)^{5 / 2}}{5}-\\left(3-\\mathrm{x}^2\\right)^{3 / 2}+\\mathrm{c} \\\\ & \\mathrm{f}(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1}{5}-1+\\mathrm{c}=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}$<br/>$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{c}=0 \\\\ & \\mathrm{f}(1)=\\frac{2^{5 / 2}}{5}-2^{3 / 2} \\\\ & =2^{1 / 2}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}-2\\right) \\\\ & \\mathrm{f}(1)-\\frac{-6 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
